-- Passage rg11-story-details-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fd3f5dd4-461b-5acd-94af-e352d8c76197', 'rg11-story-details-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Recapture$q$, $q$The mist nets went up at 5:40, thirty minutes before sunrise, and by six Amara Nwosu had already freed two song sparrows and a very offended catbird. She was seventeen and in her second season at the Corvin Marsh banding station, which meant she was trusted to take birds out of the nets but not yet to put the bands on.

Felix Arriaga ran the station now. He was twenty-six, talked constantly while he worked, and wrote every measurement twice: once on the data sheet and once on the back of his hand, a habit he said he had picked up from the woman who trained him.

At 6:50 Amara carried a cloth bag to the table and set it down gently. Inside was a small yellow warbler with a band already on its leg.

Felix stopped talking. He read the number aloud, then read it again more slowly, then opened the laptop and typed it with one finger.

"Banded here," he said. "Four years ago in August. Initials I.K."

Amara knew those initials from the old data binders that filled a whole shelf of the shed. Dr. Kowalczyk had run Corvin Marsh for twenty-two seasons and had retired the winter before Amara arrived. Felix mentioned her about once an hour.

"So it has been to South America and back," Amara said. "Four times."

"At least," Felix said. "Probably to the same patch of willows every summer." He weighed the bird, measured its wing, and checked the fat under its feathers. Then he did something Amara had never seen him do. He handed her the pliers and the data sheet and stepped back from the table.

"It already has a band," she said.

"Then you do not need to put one on," he said. "Just record it. Everything. The way she would."

Amara wrote slowly, in her neatest hand. When she looked up, Felix had his phone out and was typing a long message to someone, still smiling, and not talking at all.$q$, 'draft')
