-- Passage rg11-story-details-inference-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6ca79473-7f0b-539b-8f69-1fa6e082c138', 'rg11-story-details-inference-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Echo Seller$q$, $q$At the narrowest part of Ridgeback Canyon, where the red walls leaned so close that a shout could find no way out, a fox had built a small wooden booth. A sign on the booth read ECHOES, ONE ACORN. A white line was painted on the stone in front of it.

Travelers paid. They dropped an acorn into the fox's box, stepped to the line, and shouted whatever they liked: their own names, the names of their children, the answers to riddles. The canyon gave every word back three times, each softer than the last, and the travelers went on their way pleased, having bought something they could not carry.

One autumn a young goat named Brisk came down the canyon trail alone. She read the sign, looked at the fox, and laughed.

"An echo belongs to the canyon," she said. "You cannot sell it."

"Then do not buy it," said the fox, who was oiling the hinge of his box.

Brisk walked ten steps past the booth, well beyond the white line, and shouted her own name as loudly as she could. The canyon sent it back three times, exactly as it did for everyone else. Brisk turned around, delighted, already planning to tell every animal at the river that the fox was a fraud.

But on the walk back up the trail she went slowly, and she noticed things she had hurried past on the way down. The loose stones on the steep stretch had been wedged tight with smaller stones. Someone had stretched a rope along the cliff edge where the path narrowed, and the rope was new. At the spring halfway up, a hollow log had been set to catch the water so that small animals could drink without climbing.

Brisk stood for a long time at the spring. Then she walked all the way back down to the booth.

She did not step to the white line, and she did not shout. She dropped an acorn into the box and turned to go.

The fox looked at the acorn, and then at the goat, and for once he had nothing at all to sell her.$q$, 'draft')
